## What I built

Four civic actions (`reissue_payment`, `refund_fee`, `book_appointment`, `change_address`) now reach their systems of record only through the existing Interlock gate. I added no second state machine — the facade wires civic domain objects into the engine's `Gate`, `Rule`, `Route`, `Authority`, and `Inbox`.

- **Task 1 — simulated systems of record** (`backend/civic/`): SQLite stores, standard library only, `clock=` injected, every name and log line labeled "SIMULATED".
- **Task 2 — one target per system** (`interlock/targets/civic/`), modeled on `targets/payments.py`: payment rail is tier 1 (wraps `Payments(tier=1)` in simulation, `StripeRefunds` in test-key mode, `eid` as the Idempotency-Key, Stripe test PaymentIntent documented as a stand-in); scheduler and case records are tier 2 (lookup by `eid`). Each has `apply`, `query`, and `explain(premises, eid, effect)` returning `{violations, changes, repairs}` built on `validate_premises`.
- **Task 3 — the `CivicOffice` facade** (`backend/civic/office.py`): per-action `effect(request)` and `capture(request)` that read premises from the systems of record (never the assistant), and one `Inbox` per action family over its own `Gate` and target.
- **Task 4 — the three pre-action rules** as `approvals.Rule`: `within entitlement`, `at most one reissue per period` (counts every ledger source, including a caseworker by hand), and `case eligible and active`. I verified the $4,120-on-$412 case is refused before send, naming the $412 limit and the facts read.

## Files created

- `backend/civic/__init__.py`, `case_store.py`, `ledger.py`, `scheduler_store.py`, `office.py`
- `interlock/targets/civic/__init__.py`, `payment_rail.py`, `scheduler.py`, `case_records.py`

Files modified: `pyproject.toml` (added `interlock.targets.civic` to the packages list) and the spec `tasks.md` (checkboxes). I also extended `case_store.py` with an `address_changes` log so the tier-2 case-records target can confirm an `eid`-tagged change by lookup.

